\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The SU(2) Wilson family is not closed under convolution}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let #{\chi} be the two-dimensional fundamental character
of #{SU(2)}, let Haar measure be normalized, and define
##{Z_\beta=\int \ue^{\beta\chi(g)/2}\,d\mu(g),\qquad
 k_\beta(g)=Z_\beta^{-1}\ue^{\beta\chi(g)/2}
 \quad(\beta\ge0).}
Use convolution #{(f*h)(g)=\int f(gx^{-1})h(x)\,d\mu(x)}.
There is #{\varepsilon>0} such that, for every
#{0<\beta<\varepsilon}, no #{\gamma\ge0} satisfies
#{k_\beta*k_\beta=k_\gamma}, even as an almost-everywhere
equality.}

\proof{
\p{The fundamental character is real, lies in #{[-2,2]},
and obeys #{\chi(-g)=-\chi(g)}. Its tensor square decomposes
as #{\chi^2=1+\chi_{\rm ad}}, with the adjoint character
irreducible of dimension three. Character orthogonality
from \ref{fdip-007C} therefore gives
##{\int\chi^2\,d\mu=1,\qquad
 \int\chi^4\,d\mu=2,\qquad
 \int\chi^{2m+1}\,d\mu=0.}
Indeed the second integral is the squared norm of
#{1+\chi_{\rm ad}}. Haar invariance under #{g\mapsto-g}
gives the odd moments.}

\p{For #{|\beta|\le b}, Taylor's remainder for
#{\ue^{\beta\chi /2}} after degree #{r} is bounded uniformly
in #{g} by #{\ue^b|\beta|^{r+1}/(r+1)!}. Thus integration,
the parity just proved, and division by #{Z_\beta}, which
is positive and equals one at zero, give controlled expansions
##{\begin{aligned}
 Z_\beta&=1+\beta^2/8+O(\beta^4),\\
 a_f(\beta)&=\tfrac12\int k_\beta\chi\,d\mu
             =\beta /4+O(\beta^3),\\
 a_{\rm ad}(\beta)&=\tfrac13\int k_\beta(\chi^2-1)\,d\mu
             =\beta^2/24+O(\beta^4).
 \end{aligned}}
For the adjoint numerator the coefficient before division
by three is
#{\tfrac18\int(\chi^4-\chi^2)\,d\mu=1/8}.
The remainders are bounded by constants times the stated
powers throughout a fixed neighborhood of zero.}

\p{For a central density #{k}, Schur's lemma makes
#{\int k(g)\rho(g)\,d\mu(g)} scalar. On the two real
characters used here that scalar is
#{a_\rho=d_\rho^{-1}\int k\chi_\rho\,d\mu}.
Fubini and #{g=yx} show that convolution multiplies these
operators, hence squares these two scalars for #{k*k}.
This is the normalized central-character contraction
underlying \ref{fdip-007C} and the face-merging calculation
of \citet{§2, pp. 415–416, equations (19)–(23)}{migdal1975recursion}.
Consequently equality with #{k_\gamma} requires
##{a_f(\gamma)=a_f(\beta)^2,\qquad
 a_{\rm ad}(\gamma)=a_{\rm ad}(\beta)^2.}}

\p{Differentiation under the bounded-character integral
gives, for every finite #{\beta\ge0},
##{a_f'(\beta)=\tfrac14\operatorname{Var}_{k_\beta\mu}(\chi)>0.}
The variance is positive because #{\chi} is nonconstant
and the strictly positive density has full Haar support.
Since #{a_f(0)=0} and #{\chi<2} on an open set of positive
measure, #{0<a_f(\beta)<1} for #{\beta>0}.
There is therefore exactly one solution of the fundamental
equation among nonnegative couplings, and it lies in #{(0,\beta)}.
Existence follows by the intermediate value theorem;
strict monotonicity gives uniqueness.}

\p{As #{\beta\to0}, this solution #{\gamma} tends to zero.
The first expansion and its nonzero derivative imply first
#{\gamma=O(\beta^2)}, and then
##{\gamma=\beta^2/4+O(\beta^4).}
Substitute into the adjoint expansion:
##{\begin{aligned}
 a_{\rm ad}(\beta)^2&=\beta^4/576+O(\beta^6),\\
 a_{\rm ad}(\gamma)&=\beta^4/384+O(\beta^6),\\
 a_{\rm ad}(\beta)^2-a_{\rm ad}(\gamma)
   &=-\beta^4/1152+O(\beta^6).
 \end{aligned}}
The remainder bound makes this strictly negative for every
sufficiently small positive #{\beta}. It contradicts the
second necessary equation. Uniqueness in the preceding
paragraph excludes every other nonnegative coupling.}
}

\p{The explicit Wilson character coefficients in
\citet{§2.3, p. 5, equations (13)–(14)}{caselle1996recent}
also give the leading coefficients. The
calculation above proves the discrepancy and its remainder
directly. It concerns this analytic SU(2) family and this
convolution operation; it is not a claim about every compact
group, action or coarse path map.}
